{"task": {"id": "I_kwDOUFmNcc8AAAABUkVYaw", "item_id": "PVTI_lAHOAHSLSM4BlSCUzg-F6EA", "project_id": "PVT_kwHOAHSLSM4BlSCU", "number": 40, "title": "Showcase: build a minimal walkable Web3D metaverse foundation", "body": "# Goal

Build a small Web3D metaverse-style showcase using the existing three-game-kit Core and public Features as much as possible.

The first milestone is intentionally small: prove that a real animated 3D avatar can inhabit and move through a compact virtual world, while keeping the state and architecture ready for later multiplayer and social features.

Do not build a full metaverse platform in this Issue.

## Expected outcome

A user can open the showcase in the browser and:

- enter a small 3D world;
- control a real 3D avatar;
- walk and run;
- jump if appropriate;
- use a third-person camera;
- see idle / walk / run animation transitions;
- interact with at least one simple world object;
- optionally display a simple avatar name/profile label;
- restart and dispose the showcase cleanly.

The result should feel like the smallest believable foundation of a virtual social world rather than a technical primitive test.

## Use existing three-game-kit capabilities first

Prefer existing public Features and APIs, including where useful:

- Character Controller;
- Animation;
- Asset Manager;
- Three Rendering;
- Third-person Camera / Camera Extensions;
- Movement / Input;
- Interaction;
- Audio;
- VFX / Particles;
- UI/HUD;
- Save/Load;
- Debug/DevTools.

Do not introduce new Core abstractions for this showcase.

## Metaverse-specific code

It is acceptable to add a small showcase-local Feature such as:

```text
metaverse.rules
```

or equivalent application code for responsibilities that are specific to this initial demo.

If a missing capability is encountered, implement the smallest local version first.

Only promote something into three-game-kit as a reusable Feature or Add-on after the showcase demonstrates a clear reusable need.

## Avatar

Use a real glTF/GLB avatar rather than a capsule, box, or primitive-only character.

Minimum presentation:

- idle animation;
- walk animation;
- run animation;
- coherent facing direction;
- grounded placement in the world.

Prefer an original or redistribution-safe asset. Record provenance and asset intake details.

## World

Keep the first world compact.

It should contain enough authored environment to feel like a place, for example:

- ground and paths;
- a few buildings or structures;
- props;
- lighting and sky;
- one or more points of interest.

Do not expand this into a large open world in this Issue.

Use proper 3D assets where they materially improve the result instead of relying only on boxes and spheres.

## Interaction

Add at least one simple interaction using the existing Interaction capability where practical.

Examples:

- activate a light;
- open a door;
- trigger an object;
- sit/stand interaction;
- inspect a point of interest.

The specific interaction is less important than proving that avatar movement and world interaction compose cleanly.

## Future multiplayer readiness

This Issue does **not** require multiplayer.

However, structure important state so that later work can reasonably introduce:

- Presence / Rooms;
- remote avatar replication;
- avatar profile/appearance;
- persistent world state;
- text or spatial voice chat.

Do not implement these speculatively in this Issue.

Gameplay/world state should not depend unnecessarily on presentation-only Three.js objects.

## AI / QA mode

Preserve the repository's deterministic testing style.

Provide a test/debug surface that can at minimum:

- start/reset the showcase;
- inject semantic movement;
- advance deterministic time where practical;
- inspect avatar position and movement state;
- inspect current animation state;
- inspect interaction state;
- inspect runtime errors;
- dispose cleanly.

## Non-goals

Do not add in this Issue:

- multiplayer;
- matchmaking;
- Presence / Room infrastructure;
- generic replication;
- spatial voice;
- text chat;
- friends/social graph;
- user-generated world editing;
- persistent shared-world backend;
- moderation/permissions system;
- a generalized metaverse framework;
- speculative Core changes.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A browser showcase exists for the minimal metaverse experience.
- [ ] A real 3D avatar loads through public asset APIs.
- [ ] The avatar can walk and run through the world.
- [ ] Idle / walk / run animations are visibly integrated.
- [ ] Third-person camera behavior is usable.
- [ ] The world contains authored 3D environment content beyond primitive-only placeholder geometry.
- [ ] At least one world interaction works.
- [ ] Existing public three-game-kit Features are used where they already cover the responsibility.
- [ ] Any metaverse-specific logic remains local unless reusable need is demonstrated.
- [ ] The implementation remains structurally ready for later multiplayer without implementing it now.
- [ ] Deterministic/debug inspection covers the main avatar and interaction path.
- [ ] Restart/disposal leaves no unexpected runtime errors or owned-resource leaks.
- [ ] Relevant build/typecheck/browser verification passes.
